FactDisjointness: a core rule refuses a domain whose rules declare fact types related by derivation (GitHub #63, constraint 4)

Dev accepted, in the words "Accepted, build it", the recommendation brought after the #47 close. OWNING ISSUE:
https://github.com/ejb816/nexonix/issues/63, "Typed message dispatch" — its November 4 status lists what remains of
the design: payload and tag relationships, closedness, and "an inheritance-related fact-set lint". This commit is
the lint, constraint 4 of the issue; the grammar points stay open, so the issue stays open.

WHY. SubtypeFactVisibilityTest's finding is the one categorical row left in the DRACO.md table: when one Evrete
knowledge declares two fact types from one hierarchy, the engine resolves neither, skips the insert and logs a
warning — nothing throws, both rules stay quiet, a transform simply does not happen (§6). The corpus was safe only
by shape, MEASURED today over the whole corpus before recommending: 25 domains, 9 with rules, 23 rules, and no domain
whose declared fact types are related by derivation — each medium two siblings, Draco a disjoint taxonomy
(Problem / TypeDefinition / DomainType / DomainOntology), the Editor six request types whose shared parents Latent and
Request no rule declares. Nothing guarded that shape; the next rule written over Request beside the intent rules
would have disabled the Editor silently.

THE DESIGN, accepted: the guarantee is a definition-time rule owned by draco the way Uniqueness is, not a runtime
check. `draco.FactDisjointness` joins `var d DomainType` with `var o DomainOntology` in the validation session the
Editor fires; a Problem names the domain and every pair in which one declared fact type is an ancestor of another
through the ontology's derivation edges. A rule's variable type is text, read as an identity through the member's
resolution record: a dotted name is its path, a bare name is looked up in `uses` by simple name, else own-package —
the record's purpose (2026-10-21), the reading AssemblyValidator applies in mods, now in core where main may not call
the mods resolver. REJECTED: a lint in DomainBuilder's hand-written validate (the Editor does not run it); a check at
knowledge construction (after acceptance).

BUILT. The helpers sit where their data is, no engine change: TypeDefinition gains two globals beside isStub —
`identity(td, text)` (the record reading above; the split is `text.split("[.]")`, a character class — no escaped
backslash in a drake string and no host class name in a definition body) and `factTypes(td)`, the fact types a rule member's pattern variables declare, a
non-string value type (a form tree) skipped. DomainOntology gains `ancestors(typeName)`, a recursive dyn over
`derivations` compared by namePath, and `relatedFactTypes(domain)`, the pairs `(fact, ancestor)` among the distinct
fact types of the domain's rule members. FactDisjointness.drake is the rule: `con o.relatedFactTypes(d).nonEmpty`,
the action binding the pairs as `"X derives Y"` strings and inserting one Problem for the domain. Draco lists it as a
member (its actor's knowledge registers it with the other member rules); the Editor records it in `uses` and fires it
beside the five core rules. Five JSON re-canonicalized by `DrakeCLI parse`, five Scala regenerated by `GeneratorCLI
generate`, `DrakeCLI check` clean over main and mods. The rule passes its own lint: it declares DomainType and
DomainOntology, both already declared in Draco's session, unrelated. FactDisjointnessRulesTest (3) is new: zero
Problems over the canonical ontology; siblings pass while a type and its parent are refused with one Problem naming
the domain and "example.lint.C derives example.lint.A"; the record wins over the own package for a bare name, a
dotted name is its path, a plain type declares nothing. One corpus type added: parse scope 111 + 73, surface losses
0 across 184 types, 111 aspect sections, GenDrake 109 of 109, DracoGenTest 111 main.

DIRECT RUN of every discovered suite on compiled classes with current main, mods and test resources on the loader roots
(the regenerated files and the new suite compiled into the overlay, test classes on the Runner's runpath alone). THE FIRST
RUN FAILED ELEVEN TESTS, 1116 / 11, three causes, all in the increment: (1) the split had been written
`text.split(java.util.regex.Pattern.quote("."))`, and `java.util.regex.Pattern` is a host name the HostBindings vocabulary
does not carry, so every path through NameResolution over TypeDefinition's body — the two-stage loader (DomainLoadingTest ×3,
ExampleLoadingTest ×2), the compact fixpoint and the unscoped-equals-scoped assertion (ResolutionRecordTest ×2), the scoped
DracoGenTest gate and GenerateAndCompileTest for TypeDefinition — refused it; the character class `"[.]"` replaces it, no
host name in the body. (2) EditorTest counts the rules the Editor's start block registers: ten became eleven; the assertion
and its message say six core rules. (3) Editor.scala had been generated while the overlay was mid-update and spelled the
new line bare where the canonical scope spells it `draco.FactDisjointness`, as it spells the other core rules; regenerated
on the settled overlay, unscoped and scoped agree and the committed line is the full spelling. Focused reruns green
(DomainLoadingTest, ResolutionRecordTest, EditorTest, ExampleLoadingTest, GenerateAndCompileTest, DracoGenTest: 240 / 0;
EditorTest + ResolutionRecordTest: 42 / 0). SECOND RUN:
1127 tests / 79 suites including discovery, zero failed, canceled, ignored, pending or aborted, green; every headline as expected — example gate 48 / 0, surface losses 0 across 184 types, parse scope 111 + 73 with 0 held back, scenario 23 clean, GenDrake 109 of 109, both aspect probes 111 complete, the PON pair 42 and 7 as suspended, the rete finding categorical; the eight new tests are the five per-type gates for FactDisjointness and the three of FactDisjointnessRulesTest; the tree afterwards held the intended changes only.

